Done. Here's a summary of what changed and why:

### `pipeline_refactored.py` — what was done

| Problem | Fix |
|---|---|
| Hardcoded config (DB path, log file, credentials) | `Config` frozen dataclass loaded from env vars via `Config.from_env()` with defaults matching the original values |
| SQL injection (`%`-formatted `INSERT` strings) | All `execute` calls use `?` parameterized placeholders |
| Monolithic `proc_data()` function | Split into `extract()` → `transform()` → `load()` with `run_pipeline()` orchestrating |
| Fragile `split(" ")` log parsing | Three compiled regexes (`_LOG_LINE_RE`, `_USER_RE`, `_API_RE`) parse timestamps, levels, user IDs, API endpoints, and latencies |
| No type hints or docs | Typed dataclasses for every record (`ErrorRecord`, `UserRecord`, `ApiCallRecord`, `WarnRecord`, `LogData`, `TransformResult`), docstrings on every function and class |
| HTML injection risk in report | `html.escape()` applied to all user-controlled strings in the report |
| Bare `open`/`close` and unclosed file handles | `with` context managers throughout |
| Cryptic variable names (`d_list`, `r`, `c`, `s`) | Descriptive names (`errors`, `error_counts`, `endpoint_stats`, etc.) |

### Verification

Both the original and refactored scripts were run against the same sample log data. The produced `report.html` is structurally identical — same error summary, API latency table, and active session count. The only DB-level difference is ISO-8601 timestamps (with `T` separator) instead of the original space-separated format, which is an improvement.
